-- Passage rg3-argument-evidence-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('743f1cbd-9fd6-54a3-a724-aa724342cd46', 'rg3-argument-evidence-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g3-argument-evidence'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Two Ways Across the River$q$, $q$The town of Millbrook sits on both sides of a wide river. For many years, people crossed the river in two ways. Some took the ferry, and some used the bridge.

The ferry is a flat boat that carries people and cars. It crosses the river every thirty minutes. Riders can stand on the deck and watch ducks and fishing boats. However, the ferry cannot run when the river freezes in winter.

The bridge is made of steel and stone. It was built about fifty years ago. Cars can cross it at any time of day or night. Unlike the ferry, the bridge stays open all winter. On the other hand, there is no time to look around, because the cars move quickly.

Both the ferry and the bridge help people get to school, work and the market. Both connect the two halves of the town. Many families in Millbrook use the bridge on busy weekdays. They save the ferry for slow, sunny weekends.$q$, 'draft')
