-- Passage rg3-figurative-language-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('951e3b3f-1f68-54f1-a87b-efa8aeefa0d4', 'rg3-figurative-language-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g3-figurative-language'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Warm in the Water$q$, $q$Sea otters live in cold ocean water along rocky coasts. Seals and whales have a thick layer of fat to keep them warm, but sea otters do not. Instead, they wear a coat of fur. In fact, sea otters have the thickest fur of any animal on Earth.

A sea otter spends hours each day keeping its coat in tip-top shape. It rubs, combs and fluffs its fur with its paws. This traps tiny bubbles of air next to its skin, which helps keep the cold water out.

Sea otters are smart eaters, too. An otter dives to the sea floor and grabs a clam or a crab. Then it floats on its back and uses its belly as a table. To crack a hard shell, it bangs the shell against a rock resting on its chest.

A baby otter is called a pup. Its fluffy fur holds so much air that it bobs on the water like a cork. While the mother dives for food, the pup floats safely on the surface.

When sea otters rest, some wrap themselves in long strands of seaweed so they do not drift away. Others hold paws with a neighbor. A group of otters resting together is called a raft. These furry swimmers are the champions of staying warm in the sea.$q$, 'draft')
