-- Passage rg3-figurative-language-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c8ece9ac-7bba-581b-931b-cf7b35675dff', 'rg3-figurative-language-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g3-figurative-language'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Night Hunters$q$, $q$When the sun goes down, most birds tuck themselves away to sleep. But many owls are just waking up. These birds are night owls in more ways than one!

Owls are built for hunting in the dark. Their huge eyes gather what little light there is. An owl can also turn its head very far to look behind it. This helps it spot a mouse scurrying across a field.

Hearing is an owl's secret weapon. Some owls have one ear a little higher than the other. This helps them figure out exactly where a sound is coming from. An owl can even hear a mouse moving under the snow.

Owls fly almost without a sound. Their feathers have soft, fringed edges that hush the noise of their wings. This lets an owl glide close to a mouse before the mouse hears anything.

During the day, owls rest in trees, barns or holes. Their brown and gray feathers help them blend in with the bark. You could walk right past one and never notice it.

Scientists who study owls say these birds are a big help to farmers. One owl family can eat thousands of mice in a year. That keeps the mice from gobbling up the farmers' crops.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
