-- Passage rg3-literary-structure-pov-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('439aa76d-b98a-5180-bdd0-139c61b45fc8', 'rg3-literary-structure-pov-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g3-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Beaver and Heron$q$, $q$Beaver worked all spring on a dam across Willow Creek. Stick by stick and log by log, the dam grew tall. Behind it, a deep pond spread out wide and still. Beaver swam in circles and smiled. "What a fine pond I have made," Beaver said.

Below the dam, Heron stood in the creek on long, thin legs. The water there had shrunk to a trickle, and the little fish had gone. Heron flew up to the dam. "Friend Beaver, please let a little water pass. I cannot catch my supper in a creek this thin." Beaver slapped the pond with a flat tail. "I built this dam, so this water is mine. Go find your own pond."

Then the summer rains came. Day after day, rain filled the pond higher and higher. With no gap in the dam, the water had nowhere to go. One morning Beaver woke up in a soggy lodge, with water dripping through the roof.

Beaver thought for a long time. Then Beaver pulled three sticks from the top of the dam. Water spilled over the gap and rushed down the creek. The pond settled back to a safe level, and soon little fish were flashing around Heron's feet again.

"Thank you, Beaver," Heron called.

"Thank the rain," Beaver said. "It taught me that a creek is for everyone who lives along it."

A creek that is shared serves everyone well.$q$, 'draft')
